KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp26
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ a call satisfying the precondition: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.precondition_instance\.2\] line \d+ precondition x > 0: FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
With the enforce evaluation semantic ([basic.contract.eval]/4: a contract
violation invokes the contract-violation handler, then the program is
terminated), as GCC 16 -fcontracts uses by default, f(0) violates its
precondition and the program ends there: the precondition is the one
property that fails.  CBMC ignored pre() and post(); in C++26 mode it now
enforces them (the precondition is checked at each call site, by
goto-programs' instrument_preconditions).  Checked with GCC 16 -fcontracts
(f(0) aborts).
